+# Performance review: `searchFiles` (round 0)
+
+Scope: work that grows badly with input, needless I/O, memory. README.md:30-32
+sets the load profile: up to thousands of documents of a few megabytes each,
+called on every request, no cache.
+
+## Major
+
+1. **src/store.mjs:58 — every search reads and UTF-8-decodes every file in the
+   folder, synchronously, on every request.**
+   `fileNames(dir).filter((name) => readUserFile(dir, name).includes(query))`
+   is O(total bytes in the folder) per request: for the documented upper end
+   (thousands of files x a few MB) that is gigabytes of disk I/O plus full
+   UTF-8 -> JS-string decoding per search, all via `readFileSync`, which
+   blocks the event loop of the HTTP layer for the whole scan. `includes`
+   then rescans the decoded string. There is no early exit, no size bound,
+   no streaming, no index. The task says "build on the store's existing
+   helpers", so a full scan is the expected baseline, but this is the cost
+   that will dominate and should be recorded: at minimum consider reading
+   as a Buffer and using `Buffer.includes(Buffer.from(query))` to skip the
+   decode (the query is plain-substring, so byte search is equivalent for
+   valid UTF-8), and consider an async/streaming read so a single large
+   user's search does not stall every other request.
+
+2. **src/store.mjs:24 — `listFiles` reads every file in full just to learn
+   its size, immediately after `fileNames` (line 18) already `stat`ed it.**
+   `fileNames` calls `statSync` per entry and discards the `Stats` object
+   (keeping only `isFile()`); `listFiles` then does `readFileSync(...).length`
+   per file. That is one full read of every document per listing (again
+   gigabytes at the documented upper end) when `stats.size` was already in
+   hand. This behaviour predates the change, but the diff rewrote this line
+   and introduced the helper that throws the size away, so it is the place to
+   fix it: have `fileNames` (or a sibling) return `{ name, size }` from the
+   stat, and drop the read.
+
+## Minor
+
+3. **src/store.mjs:17-18 — `readdirSync` followed by a `statSync` per entry.**
+   `readdirSync(dir, { withFileTypes: true })` returns `Dirent`s whose
+   `isFile()` answers the same question with no extra syscall, cutting
+   N stat syscalls per call (N = entries in the folder). Note that for
+   symlinked entries `Dirent.isFile()` is false where `statSync` follows the
+   link, so the semantics differ slightly; if following symlinked files is
+   required, keep `statSync` but reuse its `size` (see finding 2).
+
+4. **src/store.mjs:42 — two `realpathSync` calls per search with `within`.**
+   `realpathSync(root)` and `realpathSync(dir)` each walk every path
+   component. Fixed cost, small relative to finding 1; noted for
+   completeness. Could be reduced to one by checking that
+   `realpathSync(dir)` starts under `realpathSync(root)` computed once, but
+   both are needed for correctness, so this is at most a cache opportunity.
+
+5. **src/store.mjs:19 — `.sort()` on names.** O(N log N) string sort per call
+   is negligible next to the I/O; not a problem, listed only to state it was
+   considered.
+
+## Not a finding
+
+- Memory: files are read one at a time inside `filter` (line 58), so peak
+  memory is one document's UTF-8 buffer plus its decoded string (up to ~3x
+  the file size for non-ASCII text), not the whole folder. Acceptable for
+  "a few megabytes".
+- Tests (tests/store.test.mjs) use tiny fixtures; no performance test exists,
+  but none was asked for.
+
+## Verdict
+
+No blocker. Two major findings, both about full-file reads that scale with
+total bytes stored (searchFiles by design, listFiles needlessly), and three
+minor.
